Core FeaturesPrivate Assets and Media
Private assets and media jobs

Cortex stores private bytes in Convex-owned storage. The application upload and authenticated delivery limit is 20,000,000 decoded bytes. Protocol/backend/provider-input limits can be smaller; an otherwise valid file is not automatically eligible for a model input.

Service setup​

Install the authenticated HTTP routes, configure the same installation's explicit HTTP action origin and grant storage:read/storage:write as required. Image/video generation also needs qualified Gateway models, fixed supported options, model budgets and signed callback configuration. Convex must enable Gateway async-video access for the deployment before video generation is used. Text runs and private file storage work independently of that enablement.

The image profile accepts a text prompt. The supported video profile can use an authorized immutable PNG input subject to its smaller provider-input limit. This release does not provide image understanding, OCR or visual embeddings.

Upload and preview​
import type { CortexRuntimeClient } from '@cortexmemory/sdk/runtime';

export async function previewUpload(client: CortexRuntimeClient, requestId: string, file: File) {
  const asset = await client.uploadAsset(file, {
    requestId,
    mimeType: file.type || 'application/octet-stream',
    filename: file.name,
  });
  const preview = await client.createAssetUrl({ assetId: asset.assetId, version: asset.assetVersion });
  return { asset, preview, close: () => preview.revoke() };
}


Use { assetId, version } to select an immutable version. Metadata includes hash, size, MIME, lineage and optional delivery expiry. The client freshly authorizes downloads through assetHttpUrl, checks size/MIME/hash and can create a local Blob preview. Revoke the preview on close/unmount. Client close and authentication changes revoke owned previews too.

There are no public storage links, external media storage or embedded base64 transcripts. Never store provider URLs, bytes or credentials in reconnect state.

Generate and recover​
import type { CortexRuntimeClient, RuntimeAssetReference } from '@cortexmemory/sdk/runtime';

export async function generateVideo(
  client: CortexRuntimeClient,
  requestId: string,
  prompt: string,
  inputAsset?: RuntimeAssetReference,
) {
  return client.submitMediaJob({ requestId, kind: 'video', prompt, inputAsset });
}


submitMediaJob saves a pending job and artifact before generation. The backend selects the qualified model/options; callers cannot override them. Read retained state with getMediaJob, listMediaJobs and getArtifact.

Signed callbacks are verified, deduplicated and ordered against the saved upstream operation. reconcileMediaJob polls/downloads that existing acknowledged operation and can recover a missed callback. submission_unknown retains uncertainty; reconnect, polling or replay never blindly submits another generation.

cancelMediaJob fences local delivery. upstreamCancellation: 'not_supported' means the provider can continue and incur cost. costStatus distinguishes reserved, incurred-unknown and settled cost. A local deadline does not prove a terminal upstream outcome or justify refund/retry.

Revocation, deletion/tombstones, artifact history and retained-reference cleanup govern files and background jobs. The runtime client API lists asset, content and media methods.

